# English code, Chinese UI copy (i18n source)

**MANDATORY.** This repository keeps one consistent language for *code* and one for *content*. The split is not a
weakening of the rule; it is the rule.

- **Code and metadata -> English.** Identifiers, file names, comments, JSDoc, test names, configuration keys, CSS
  class names, route paths, API contracts, documentation headings, rule bodies and commit messages.
- **User-facing copy -> Simplified Chinese (zh-Hans).** Button labels, navigation and menu items, page and section
  headings, form labels, placeholders, tooltips and `aria-label`s, empty states, table headers, toast and banner
  messages, validation messages. These are the **i18n source strings** for `sourceLocale: "zh-Hans"`, not technical
  debt. The English UI is delivered by the `en-US` translation catalog, not by writing English into templates.

This keeps the codebase grep-able and reviewable on GitHub for international maintainers while giving the product a
real Chinese default locale and a complete English variant.

## Scope: git-tracked files only

- In scope: source, templates, styles, tests, docs, `.mdc` rules, configuration, comments, identifiers, UI copy and
  commit messages that land in the repository.
- Determine scope with `git ls-files` and `git status --short`. A tracked file being modified (`M`) or a file staged for
  addition (`A`) is in scope.
- **Exempt**: local temporary files that are not under version control — anything ignored
  by [.gitignore](mdc:.gitignore) or a parent `.gitignore` (e.g. `node_modules/`, `dist/`, `.angular/`, local logs,
  screenshots, scratch notes, IDE-local plans and user-level config). They may use any language and must never be added
  to the repository just to satisfy this rule. Use `git check-ignore -v <path>` when in doubt.
- A file becomes in-scope the moment it moves from ignored/untracked into git: apply the code/content split **before**
  staging it.

## Code layer (must be English)

- Identifiers, file names, comments and JSDoc, `describe` / `it` test names, commit messages, documentation headings,
  rule bodies, configuration keys, CSS class names, route paths and API contracts.
- Machine-readable values stay English: HTTP status codes, error codes, RFC7807 field names and types, log messages.
  Only the display string rendered to a user may be Chinese.
- Allowed exceptions: proper nouns, brand names, third-party URLs, snippets quoted verbatim from external documentation,
  and data values that must match an external system.

## Content layer (may and should be Chinese)

- Any string rendered to the user, including button text, menu items, headings, labels, placeholders, tooltips,
  aria-labels, empty states, table headers, toast / banner messages and validation messages.
- Every such string must be marked for translation — an `i18n` attribute (or `i18n-*` for attributes) in templates, or
  `$localize` in TypeScript — so `ng extract-i18n` can collect it into the message catalog.
- Chinese strings held in TypeScript data (arrays, menu definitions, column headers, constants) are i18n source text
  too, and must be wrapped in `$localize`.
- Translation catalogs (`src/locale/messages.*.xlf` and equivalents) are content resources: the `zh-Hans` source
  messages and any Chinese targets are expected and are **not** violations.
- Test fixtures that intentionally contain Chinese characters (e.g. verifying UTF-8 Basic auth encoding) are data
  values and stay as-is.

## Handling existing English UI copy

- English UI strings currently sitting in tracked templates are **migration debt, not the target state**. When a file is
  touched for i18n work, convert its user-facing strings to Chinese source text, add translation markers, and keep its
  code and comment layer English.
- Do **not** add new English UI copy once i18n is in place. Translating a test title or a comment to English is still
  correct — test names and comments belong to the code layer.
- A full translation pass of untouched files is a separate, explicitly requested task.

## Why

A repository shared on GitHub is read by international maintainers. Mixed Chinese/English identifiers, comments and
commit messages hurt grep-ability, tooling and AI context, and make reviews harder. UI copy is different: it is content,
and with Angular i18n it belongs in a marked source catalog plus a translation catalog. Keeping that content in Chinese
for the base locale gives the product an honest `zh-Hans` default instead of a declared-but-unused one.

## Verification before delivery

- `git status --short` shows only the intended files.
- For each changed tracked file, grep for CJK characters (`[\u4e00-\u9fff]`). Every hit must fall in the **content
  layer** above (UI source copy, translation catalogs, or intentional test data) — never in identifiers, comments, test
  names, log messages or commit messages.
- Confirm every newly added UI string carries an `i18n` marker or `$localize`, and that `ng extract-i18n` collects the
  expected message count.

